  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nLatest analysis showers the main axis of low-level warm\nadvection (around the 925mb height) that has been the focus for\nheavy rain shower and thunderstorm development NE of it is\nstruggling to advance farther east than the IN/OH border. Add in\ngenerally mostly cloudy skies for the region that limits the\ndegree of destabilization, the risk for flash flooding is\nlowering as the next wave is more likely to produce light to\nmoderate rain showers mainly W of I-79 and south of I-80. The\nthreat remains non-zero given prior morning rain that saturated\nvarious localities and the continued presence of 95th+\npercentile PWATs, but the inability to foster additional\ndevelopment while maximizing their rainfall rates favors the\nlack of additional flooding concerns.\n\nSurface high pressure behind the shortwave driving the OH\nconvection currently will continue to shift north of the region\nthrough Friday morning. This will push the nominal surface\nboundary southward and offer slightly cooler plus drier air for\nthe rest of the day Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe reprieve in active weather will be short-lived as there is\nhigh confidence for more rounds of showers and thunderstorms\nstarting Saturday into early next week. This is due to the\nregion generally sitting on the NE periphery of a lower\nMississippi to Tennessee River Valley high while a series of\nshortwaves traverse the ridge top. Additionally, warm, moist\nadvection favors a return of the humid airmass characterized by\nanomalously high PWAT values ranging from 1.4\"-1.9\", all of \nwhich is above the 90th percentile. Though finer details vary \nmodel-to-model and day-to-day (some discussed momentarily), the \nbroader messaging of this pattern lends to likely periods \nfeaturing showers/thunderstorm that carry an increased risk for\nflash flooding and, pending other environmental aspects, severe\nweather.\n\nOne component that can be expounded on more is the flash flood\nrisk for the Saturday night to Sunday night 24-hour period. For\nreference, global models (notoriously weak on convective element\ndepictions) suggest a setup that sees an MCS exiting the western\nGreat Lakes towards the Upper Ohio River Valley producing a\nlarge swath of rain. Current ensemble mean rainfall total values\nover this 24-hour period range from 0.75\"-1.5\" with the axis\nfavoring the I-80 corridor. Pending the potential north/south\nre-positioning of this axis, high-end scenarios could see totals\nin the heaviest axis in the 2-3\" range; any additional\nconvective element boost theoretically could push that higher.\nEntering this period, the region already is fairly saturated\nwith lowered flash flood guidance; any rain that develops AFTER\nthis initial window likely does with almost fully saturated\ngrounds that sees a high percentage of rainfall translate to\nrun-off (increasing flash flood likelihood). If confidence in\nthese outcomes increase, Flood Watches may be issued in the\ncoming days.",
